262 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 599 0 R
/E (The Translation Crash: JSON is very basic and only understands simple things like text, numbers, and lists. It has no idea what a Python date, set, or custom object is. If you try to save them directly, your code will crash. You must simplify them first: turn dates into text strings, and turn sets into normal lists.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
263 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 600 0 R
/E (The Sneaky Key Change: Python lets you use numbers as dictionary keys \(like {1: "apple"}\). JSON absolutely refuses this and only allows text keys. It will quietly change your number 1 into a text string "1" without warning you. When you load your data back into Python, you might get bugs because your numbers are now text.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
264 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 601 0 R
/E (The Broken Data Trap: Never blindly trust a JSON file from the internet or a user. If the file is missing even a single comma or quote, your entire program will instantly crash when it tries to read it. Always wrap your code in a try/except block so it catches the error instead of breaking your app.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
265 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 602 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
266 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 603 0 R
/E (The Windows vs. Mac Glitch: Different computers assume different default text formats when opening files. If you do not specify a format, moving a JSON file between computers can scramble the text. Always explicitly write encoding="utf-8" inside your open\(\) function to guarantee it works flawlessly everywhere.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
